<div class="research-stack" role="figure" aria-label="Research across the AI system stack">
  <div class="rs-apps">
    <span class="rs-caption">Applications</span>
    <span class="rs-chip">Vision</span>
    <span class="rs-chip">Language</span>
    <span class="rs-chip">Embodied AI</span>
    <span class="rs-chip">Biosignals</span>
  </div>
  <div class="rs-body">
    <div class="rs-axis" aria-hidden="true">
      <span>Co-design</span>
      <i class="rs-arrow-down"></i>
    </div>
    <div class="rs-layers">
      <div class="rs-layer" id="research-models" style="--rs-tint: 7%">
        <div class="rs-head"><strong>Efficient model design</strong><span>Transformers, state-space models, foundation models</span></div>
        <p class="rs-text">
          what is the right computational mechanism for a given architecture? I have studied this for
          <a href="/assets/pdf/2019_iccv_sgn.pdf">CNNs</a>, <a href="/assets/pdf/2021_iccv_efficient_gcn.pdf">graph neural networks</a>, vision
          Transformers (<a href="/assets/pdf/2023_iros_localvit.pdf">LocalViT</a>, <a href="/assets/pdf/2023_cvpr_grl.pdf">GRL</a>,
          <a href="/assets/pdf/2024_neurips_semanir.pdf">SemanIR</a>, <a href="/assets/pdf/2024_tip_vrt.pdf">VRT</a>), and state-space models (<a href="/assets/pdf/2025_cvpr_mambairv2.pdf"
            >MambaIR</a
          >, <a href="/assets/pdf/2025_embc_femba.pdf">FEMBA</a>).
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="rs-layer" id="research-compression" style="--rs-tint: 11%">
        <div class="rs-head">
          <strong>Model compression &amp; deployment</strong><span>Quantization, pruning, low-rank adaptation, token pruning</span>
        </div>
        <p class="rs-text">
          how much of a trained model is actually needed? My work spans weight pruning (<a href="/assets/pdf/2020_cvpr_group_sparsity.pdf"
            >group sparsity</a
          >, <a href="/assets/pdf/2022_cvpr_random_pruning.pdf">random pruning</a>, <a href="/assets/pdf/2020_eccv_dhp.pdf">DHP</a>), token pruning (<a href="/assets/pdf/2025_iccv_fastvar.pdf"
            >FastVAR</a
          >), quantization (<a href="/assets/pdf/2025_icml_slim-llm.pdf">SliM-LLM</a>, <a href="/assets/pdf/2026_iclr_obr.pdf">OBR</a>), tensor
          decomposition (<a href="/assets/pdf/2019_iccv_filter_basis.pdf">learning filter basis</a>), and low-rank adaptation (<a href="/assets/pdf/2025_icml_intlora.pdf"
            >IntLoRA</a
          >), applied to LLMs and diffusion models.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="rs-layer" id="research-codesign" style="--rs-tint: 15%">
        <div class="rs-head">
          <strong>Software&ndash;hardware co-design</strong><span>Dataflow, kernels, and scheduling from clusters to the edge</span>
        </div>
        <p class="rs-text">
          once the model is fixed, the bottleneck moves to how it is mapped onto silicon. I work on serving modern models efficiently both on large
          clusters (<a href="/assets/pdf/2025_isvlsi_flatattention.pdf">FlatAttention</a>) and on severely constrained edge devices (<a href="/assets/pdf/2024_eccv_smart_glasses.pdf"
            >smart glasses</a
          >, <a href="/assets/pdf/2024_cvpr_retina.pdf">Retina</a>, <a href="/assets/pdf/2023_sensors_TinyTracker.pdf">TinyTracker</a>).
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="rs-layer" id="research-hardware" style="--rs-tint: 19%">
        <div class="rs-head">
          <strong>Hardware architecture &amp; EDA</strong><span>RISC-V processors, AI accelerators, PPA optimization, 3D integration</span>
        </div>
        <p class="rs-text">
          open-source processor and AI accelerator architectures based on RISC-V, as well as EDA methodologies for hardware PPA (Power, Performance,
          Area) optimization and 3D integration. This is the newest direction in the lab, growing out of my work at ETH Z&uuml;rich with Prof. Luca
          Benini's group.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="rs-layer rs-app" id="research-biosignals">
    <div class="rs-head"><strong>Foundation models for biosignals</strong><span>Application focus &middot; EEG, EMG, ECG, ultrasound</span></div>
    <p class="rs-text">
      biosignals record physiological activity and underpin healthcare, disease monitoring, BCI, HCI and AR/VR. They demand accurate modelling under a
      tight on-device compute budget — here efficiency is not an optimization but a precondition. This is my most active recent direction (<a href="/assets/pdf/2025_neurips_physiowave.pdf"
        >PhysioWave</a
      >, <a href="/assets/pdf/2025_neurips_luna.pdf">LUNA</a>, <a href="/assets/pdf/2025_ner_waveformer.pdf">WaveFormer</a>,
      <a href="/assets/pdf/2025_embc_femba.pdf">FEMBA</a>, <a href="/assets/pdf/2025_embc_finetuning.pdf">CEReBrO</a>).
    </p>
  </div>
</div>
